I got into film really by accident a  fortunate sense a fortunate series of  events I wanted to become a diplomat  because I had been born my parents are  both American but I was born abroad and  I grew up amongst a more European  community and when I went back to the  United States when I was fifteen I was a  bit shocked by it and I decided that I  would study to become a diplomat so I  took what we call political science and  the Russian language and I passed all  the exams for the State Department which  is your foreign service and but they  said I was way too liberal politically  to be happy at that job we were  protesting the Vietnam War and  supporting Martin Luther King in the  south and they thought that I would be  unhappy they were probably right so I  went back to do some graduate work but I  then saw an ad in the New York Times and  this never happens for someone from Los  Angeles saying that he wanted to train a  film editor in New York and you will  never see that ad again I answered the  ad and he was butchering the great films  of Fellini Truffaut Visconti from Europe  for late-night television slots because  he wanted them to fit in between 2:00  a.m. and 4:00 a.m. and so here we just  take a whole reel out of a movie like  Rocco and his brothers a great movie by  Visconti and I was horrified by what he  was doing I said you can't do that and  he said well nobody's looking at these  movies at 2:00 in the morning but Martin  Scorsese was and unknown to me and so I  was disgusted by him and I left the job  but I saw an ad for a New York  University film school just a six weeks  course in the summer and I thought I can  just barely afford that so I took it I  wasn't on Scorsese's student film  project I was on another one but someone  had butchered he is a negative of his  student film and because I had learned a  little bit about negative cutting on  this awful job the professor asked is  there anyone here  can help him restore his film and I said  well I'll try and that's how I  accidentally met him so it was an  incredible series of fateful events  luckily for me that occurred and once I  met him and he saw that he could trust  me I think to do what's right for his  movies we began to work together and  fifty years later I'm still working for  her thankfully I had seen some classic  films on a wonderful program in in  America called million dollar movie  where they ran the same movie nine times  in one week and Scorsese was obsessed  with this channel of course and that's  where he saw the films of my late  husband Michael Powell and Emeric  Pressburger he first saw them there and  when he would see their logo which was a  target with an arrow thudding into it he  said oh this is gonna be a really  interesting movie and he would watch it  nine times until his mother said if you  don't turn that off I'm gonna kill you  and I didn't I was actually watching  those movies too at the same time but I  didn't even know who he was so it was in  it was when when I met him he truly  became the reason I became a filmmaker  he trained me he taught me everything  I've ever known about film editing and  his deep passionate love of the history  of film is something he infected me with  and then actually introduced me to my  husband Michael Powell so I've had all  the luck anyone could have in the world  it is a wonderful creative process  between the two of us reaches constantly  are exchanging ideas and doing as I said  what's right for the film you know I  know I bring a great deal to him he  relies heavily on me and it's it's not a  matter of one person having creative  control or something because when you're  in a collaboration that's going back and  forth all the time we make 200 decisions  a day when we're  and it's a joint collaboration that very  important it's the just the best job in  the world  you know his films are always different  he never repeats himself each film is a  new challenge nothing could be more  different than silence and the movie I'm  working on now the Irishman so it's just  the most wonderful job in the world I'm  so lucky to have it I've worked on 24  movies with him  III read the script only once because I  want to see what he he is bringing to  the film as he shoots it because he does  not put into the film the script a great  deal of what he's going to do in the  movie I don't want to be bound either by  a book if the film is based on the book  or the script because I know that when  he gets on the set the film starts to  expand and grow and my job first is to  look at the dailies every day that he  shot the day before and he wants me to  let him know if there's anything I feel  is wrong or which hardly ever happens  and just he wants a in my perspective so  that's why I don't go on the set very  much first of all cuz I don't have time  to go I love to be on the set because I  love watching him work with actors many  of those actors have become friends over  the years so I love to watch that but I  don't have time and also he would rather  I have a cold perspective on what has  been shot I'm not there knowing that the  Sun is going down and the horse just  fell over and the actor is sick and all  that I should not be affected by that I  should see what he got on film and so  then the most important early part of  the film is when he looks at the dailies  with me and he gives me incredible  stream of thoughts about what he has  done I write like the wind fortunately  um and so I tell him what I think he  tells me what he thinks and as he's  telling me what he thinks about each  shot he'll say I'm trying to move this  actor a little bit more to a different  performance here or I think I got it in  the six take  or I hate that shot don't ever show it  to me he's very tough on himself which  is quite remarkable  but this during that process he tells me  his conception for the film you know  what his overall feeling for the film is  and that is incredibly valuable to me so  from those notes and my own notes I  start to do the first assembly of the  film as he's shooting and then when he's  through shooting his favorite part of  filmmaking is editing and so he and I  edit the film together and it's a great  joy I can tell you and as I said it's  just a very lively wonderful interplay  of ideas all working towards the same  goal which is what is right for the  movie I think the most wonderful thing  about editing is that you're given all  this raw material and it's your job to  make choices in this case along with  Scorsese into how to focus the scene you  know when do you cut a cut to a close-up  of of an of one actor rather than the  other when do you use wide shots for  example Scorsese in a famous scene in  Goodfellas where Joe Pesci  says what's so funny about me a very  interesting thing that Marty did there  was he shot that in medium shots there  are no close-ups in that sequence  because he knew that what the reactions  of the people around Joe Pesci  and of Ray Liotta were reacting to the  day the sense of danger that was  building as Pesci just kept saying  what's so funny about what it that you  almost feel that Ray Liotta may get  killed there and the reactions are the  people around him who are laughing at  first and then stop laughing  that's why Square says he shot it in  medium shots not close-ups and that's  something students need to learn  everything doesn't have to be a close-up  you have to know how to use the medium  properly so anyway the all those  decisions we make about how shape a  sequence is it's the most incredibly  creative  act anybody can have I think I think  editors need a strong sense of  discipline a musical sense which  fortunately I had I had been drained to  play instruments in the piano so  Scorsese has a great gift for putting  music to films and I think that is very  important you have to obviously have a  gift for it but you also have to  cooperate with the other filmmakers on  your film particularly the director your  job is to get his vision up on the  screen you have to give him his chance  to do it the way he wants to first then  he's usually able to see if that doesn't  work and you come up with another  solution  sometimes when score says he comes and  I've created four different edits of a  scene for him because there are so many  options and then together we decide  which option to use but your job must be  you have to not I think let your ego  play too much of a role in this because  your job is to do what's right for the  film that's most important  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]
 

